Static asset is returning the 404 error page. Let me add a debug log inside Send-StaticAsset to see where it bails. Actually, let me reason again more carefully.

`Invoke-StaticAsset` (StaticController):
```powershell
$prefixes = @{
    '/img/' = 'img'
    '/css/' = 'css'
    '/js/'  = 'js'
}
$sub = ''
if ($RelativePath -ne '') { $sub = $RelativePath }
else {
    foreach ($prefix in $prefixes.Keys) {
        if ($Ctx.Path.StartsWith($prefix)) { $sub = $prefixes[$prefix] + $Ctx.Path.Substring($prefix.Length); break }
    }
}
if ($sub -eq '') { Send-ErrorPage 404 ...; return }
Send-StaticAsset ...
```

Hmm, `$RelativePath` param — `Invoke-StaticAsset -Ctx $ctx` leaves `$RelativePath` at its default `''`. So we go to else branch.

`$Ctx.Path` = '/css/style.css'. `'/css/style.css'.StartsWith('/css/')` → true. `$prefixes['/css/']` = 'css'. `$Ctx.Path.Substring(5)` = 'style.css'. `$sub` = 'css/style.css'. 

So `$sub` should be fine...

UNLESS the `-like` dispatch isn't hitting `Invoke-StaticAsset` at all and instead falling to the 404 fallback! That's more likely — the fallback `Send-ErrorPage -StatusCode 404 -Heading 'We could not find that page'` would render a different message. But the returned page title is "404 - Page not found" which comes from `Send-ErrorPage`'s `$titles[404] = 'Page not found'`. Both paths use Send-ErrorPage, so the title is the same for both. Let me check the body content to distinguish: the static path says 'The file you requested does not exist.' vs fallback says 'The link may be old or mistyped...'.

Let me grep the body for those.

Also — `$global:PublicDir` is set in `Initialize-App`, but the dispatcher might be reached... it's set. OK let me just check the message text.

Also note: `Start-Job` in server.ps1 creates a child runspace — irrelevant.

Let me check the body text.

