content.text:
The viewport inside the iframe is 345 (360 minus scrollbar 15). The `.nav-toggle` extends to 399 — 54px beyond. The header inner is `display:flex` with `gap:16px` and `.nav-toggle { margin-left: auto }`.

The issue: the brand takes too much space, and `.brand` has `flex-shrink: 0` so it can't shrink, pushing the toggle out.

Fix: allow the brand text to shrink/truncate on small screens, and/or reduce the header gap and container padding on phones.

Let me add a mobile refinement:
```css
@media (max-width: 520px) {
  .container { padding: 0 14px; }
  .header-inner { gap: 10px; }
  .brand { min-width: 0; }
  .brand-text { min-width: 0; }
  .brand-text strong { font-size: 0.9rem; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  .brand-text small { font-size: 0.62rem; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap; }
  .brand-mark { width: 36px; height: 36px; }
  .nav-toggle { padding: 8px 11px; }
  .nav-toggle-text { display: none; }   /* icon-only on very small phones */
}
```

Hmm, hiding the "Menu" text leaves just the bars — acceptable but less clear. Better: keep the text but shrink.

Actually the cleanest fix: `.brand { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden; }` so it shrinks, and `.brand-text { min-width: 0 }` with ellipsis on the text. Then the toggle stays within bounds.

Let me also add `.skip-link { left: -9999px }` → that's fine, it doesn't cause rightward overflow. It appears in my scan because `left < -1`, but it doesn't contribute to scrollWidth on the right. So the real offender is `.nav-toggle` at right=399.

Let me apply the header fix. Let me test at 360 after the change.

Also, I should double check other pages at mobile width (e.g., the users table collapsing, forms). Let me add a broader responsive test after the fix.

Let me write the CSS fix.

